this video was made possible by the  lovely people supporting me on patreon  hello if you've not seen one of my  videos before my name's simon i make  science videos and i just wrote my first  book if you have watched some of my  videos before then hello my name's simon  i make science videos and i just wrote  my first book it's an introduction to  and history of atmospheric science  called firmament the hidden science of  weather climate change in the air that  surrounds us available to pre-order  right now at the link down there in the  description in this video i want to  answer some questions that i've been  asked elsewhere about how and why i  wrote the book starting with how did i  get the opportunity to write a book in  the first place the answer to that  question is actually quite simple i got  an email from a publisher called hodder  and stokes and asking me if i would be  interested in writing a book and that  was something that i had anticipated  being asked in fact it's something i  kind of  engineered long-term viewers of the  channel will know that i've released a  bunch of videos about books some like  favorite textbooks or a book club or  whatever the reason i started making  those videos was because i've been a  lifelong book nerd and i've always  wanted to be an author and i figured  that a reasonably successful youtube  channel would be a good way to  springboard into writing a book and that  series was basically a multi-year play  to try and attract the attention of  book publishers and book editors and  that plan unbelievably actually seemed  to work so after receiving that email i  went to hodder and stoughton and had a  meeting where i pitched a couple of  different ideas for books that i had one  of which was firmament and they really  liked that idea and said right okay  great go away and write us a book  proposal a book proposal is a relatively  long document that formerly pitches a  book so within there there's a form that  basically asks you to fill out who you  are why we should trust you to write a  book you know what do you know about  this subject but also things like who is  the book going to appeal to and a rough  skeleton structure of what's actually  going to go in there so chat to my  chapter what you think is going to go  into the book and attached to that is a  sample of your writing so a couple of  thousand words that i think quite  frequently actually becomes part of the  introduction to the book um that  basically gives the publisher a chance  to see what your authorial voice is like  and if you're pants at writing or not  now at this point in my life when i was  asked to provide several thousand words  of a writing sample i thought i had a  pretty good idea of how i wrote because  i've written a lot over the course of my  life i wrote short stories when i was a  kid i wrote like 70 000 words of a novel  when i was a teenager and just deleted  it because i wasn't happy and then more  recently i wrote an entire phd thesis i  thought that it would be easy just to  kind of put put a bunch of words down on  the page but it turns out that that  academic writing style that i got which  is very specific to academia from from  the phd  didn't really sit with a lot of my other  writing experience particularly actually  writing for youtube when i make science  videos i normally script them word for  word and um it took a little while to  kind of combine the two into what i felt  like was the  the popular science writing version of  me and it's i guess somewhere between  those two polls of youtube and academia  what's interesting isn't that now  looking back on that writing sample it  it's very obvious to me that it was two  versions of me that were pulling in  opposite directions as the book went on  and i wrote i don't even know how many  thousands of words have been written in  total and then you know subsequently  deleted uh i feel like i found a much  more authentic version of that voice and  i feel like it's a lot more convincing  to me now  whether that's convincing to other  people still remains to be seen the book  hasn't actually been released yet what  mattered of course was that the  publisher liked the writing sample and  they sent over a contract for me to sign  and there were a couple of extra steps  here where i got a literary agent and  they renegotiated the contract and blah  blah blah blah blah but anyway i signed  a contract in i think august 2019 to  provide the book by  mid-2020 and from there i went into  research mode because  i obviously  i did a phd in atmospheric science i  know a lot about this field and i had a  skeleton structure of what i wanted to  go into the book but the process of  writing was largely a process of  researching because  there's all manner of different things  that need to go into the book that i  haven't learned as part of formal  education whether that's extra levels of  complexity that i just want to  understand before i try and explain a  concept more broadly or if it's little  factoids and bits of information like  the the illegality of weather  forecasting in england in the 19th  century for example for each topic that  i knew i'd be doing more research on i  compiled a list of good sources and  tracked them down basically most of them  are online some of them are actually in  physical places like my local library in  one or two cases several were in the  british library which i got to go and do  some research in i actually vlogged this  because i thought this experience would  be something that i could vlog and i'd  go to interesting places and meet  interesting people and  we got a pandemic instead longtime  viewers of the channel will not be at  all surprised to learn that i made a  notion database for this project and  each one of those sources got its own  page where i could take notes and each  chapter in the book had its own page in  another database where i could you know  pile in the information and compile it  and then reorder it so that i could  basically follow a basic story telling  structure which is what i do for all my  videos at some point i will do a video  on how i approach science communication  and how i think it transcends different  formats but basically i wanted to try  and tell a story within each chapter and  i followed a bullet point structure and  then brought in information from those  sources as and when it was needed for  the first real chapter in the initial  structure i actually compiled all the  information first and then wrote the  whole thing and that took way too long  and for all the following chapters i  basically sketched out the rough  structure filled in some detail and then  wrote through and as i needed to get  more information i would look it up and  then quite frequently you get to the end  and actually find that oh well this  structure doesn't work and you reorder  things around but we'll get to that  stage later all this is to say i did a  bunch of research came up with a  structure and then opened a word  document and started writing i actually  filmed that moment  right i'll see you in like 80 000 words  for those of you wondering why i didn't  use latex and i used word instead one  that's because i don't need to typeset  the book i don't need to make it look  nice i just need to provide the text and  two because i think if i used latex i  would be very tempted to include lots  and lots of equations because that's  just what i do in latex and that's not  what you do in popular science writing  so basically starting in late 2019 and  for pretty much all of 2020 i was  researching and writing the book and  this was of course somewhat uh  messed with by the pandemic partly  because originally i was going to go to  lots of places and research facilities  and archives and uh make vlogs of the  experience but also put myself a bit  more into the book and have a bit more  kind of the personal experience of you  know going into somewhere and you can  smell the the ancient pages and you know  just provide that bit of personal flavor  i guess to the text obviously that  didn't happen but also the book was  delayed because i got covered and that  completely threw out the initial plan of  submitting the initial manuscript in  june 2020 we actually had to push the uh  kind of deadline day to december 2020.  so for the entire pandemic year i was  alongside doing videos and streams and  everything like that  researching and writing the book and  just kind of gradually week by week  getting to the final goal of this  complete manuscript originally i used to  put in my daily routine that i would  write for an hour and what i found was  it was very possible to  write for an hour and not produce any  words at all because you just kind of be  researching a bit and then writing a  beer and then researching and being oh  no i actually don't feel this and then  delete it and you end up exactly where  you started the following day what i  found was a much more effective goal was  to write a hundred words that was my  goal for five days a week was to write  100 words because  writing 100 words is actually very easy  it doesn't take long at all depending on  what you're writing about but once  you've written them you think well okay  i've already kind of set up i've got a  drink i've got everything open i need  open i'm just going to keep going and  quite frequently you'd write 500 or a  thousand words in a day and eventually i  upped the goal it wasn't 100 words in a  day it was 500 words in a day and i  think in the last month and just a  semi-mad rush for the finish line i  think i had a thousand words today um  and  i i hit it you know when it becomes a  regular goal a regular part of your  routine it just it's a thing that you do  and  i did it all this is to say i submitted  a complete manuscript i think actually  about a week or two early at the end of  2020 and then i had a couple of weeks  off from the project and then the edits  began in my mind at least the editing  process takes place in three stages the  first of which is asking basically are  the chapters in the right order and the  way that we approached this was i bullet  pointed the kind of key messages of each  chapter in the book and then said right  you know do these make sense are we you  know loading the front half of the book  or the back half of the book or is it  evenly spread and what we actually found  was that the start of the book and the  end of the book were fine but the middle  was a bit muddled and we actually added  i think two chapters to make the  information a bit more palatable and to  not make it read like a textbook because  there was one chapter in particular that  was very much just like reading a  textbook and  wasn't the state of goal the second  stage is then going into each chapter  and asking are the paragraphs in the  right order are the concepts you're  introducing taking place in a sensible  way can we colony concepts do we need to  add any others do we need to change any  analogies and this process is the part  that takes the longest time um some  chapters actually didn't take very long  at all uh my editor ian just sent over  basically one round of notes for some  chapters and it was like okay cool yeah  i'll just you know move this paragraph  here just cut out that sentence add a  sentence here rubber stamp great that  one is now ready to go down the line  other chapters however such as the ones  in the middle were a lot more work  sometimes there were three or four  iterations to get them right so i would  send something to ian he would write up  notes on it send them over to me we'd  then have a video call to talk it  through i'd implement the notes with my  own kind of suggestions on top send it  back and then  repeat the process and eventually those  chapters were hammered out those ones in  the middle of the book to me are i think  some of the best chapters because  they've  been through the forge so many times  i've really hit them with a hammer and i  think got them to the right form now  hopefully right now as of the time of  releasing this video we have just  finished this second stage of editing  which may seem a little premature but  actually the third stage of editing is  going through line by line and asking is  the grammar correct is the spelling  correct that kind of stuff which  actually i don't do and my editor  doesn't do that gets sent off to a copy  editor who then returns the manuscript  with any  mistakes corrected potentially with you  know a couple of notes asking did you  mean this in which case you need to  change it to this that kind of thing but  that is hopefully going to be quite a  quick process once these three stages of  editing are done  that's pretty much the point at which my  hands are off the wheel like there is  one other step when they send over the  page proofs that i can check to see if  i'm happy with the way it looks on the  page  but at this point the book is written  and  i i've just got to kind of let my baby  go off into the world and  eventually see it in shops in a couple  of months rising this book was a long  process in fact it's almost exactly  three years from the day i received that  first email to the day that i will see  it in bookshops so it's taking a long  time and it has been difficult it's a  process that is it's difficult holding  all the information in your head about  the book whilst you're trying to edit it  but also whilst you're writing it you  necessarily only write about a fraction  of what you research you only  cherry-pick the most relevant the most  interesting bits and you have to  summarize down these large concepts into  digestible  sentences and that's a tough process  like  it was harder than i thought it would be  but it is a process that i enjoyed i  enjoy writing i enjoy researching and  writing the book has made me  so much better as a writer i feel like i  found my voice a lot more i look back on  the stuff that i wrote two a bit years  ago now and it's it's like a totally  different person wrote it um somebody  who was just clunky  and was like tripping over their own  feet trying to write something that  sounded right i feel just so much more  comfortable in the process now if i had  the opportunity to do this again in the  future i probably take it  but let's focus on getting this one  finished shall we but anyway that's how  i wrote my first book i hope that this  was at least interesting you know  looking behind the curtain a little bit  and potentially even useful if you  yourself might like to write a piece of  non-fiction in the future if you still  have any questions about the book or  about how i wrote it then i'll be  chilling out down there in the comments  for an hour or two after this video is  released now remember that supporters on  patreon get the most direct access to  ask me questions at any time in the  comments or in messages because it's the  least i can do for the people that  directly financially support this  channel thank you guys you're awesome  here's some recommended viewing next if  you enjoyed the video then please do pop  it a like and that just leaves me to say  thanks for watching i'll see in the next  one

